Q4: QUEUE THEORY 


Q4.1: What is a Queue? State the principle it follows and give two real-world 
examples (not from this assignment) where queue behaviour is observed.

A queue is a linear data structure that follows the First-In-First-Out (FIFO) 
principle. This means that the element added first is the first one to be 
removed. In a queue, insertions occur at one end called the rear (or tail) and 
deletions occur at the other end called the front (or head).

The FIFO principle works like a real-world queue at a bank or grocery store: the 
first person who joins the line is the first person to be served. People join 
at the back of the queue and are served from the front.

Two real-world examples where queue behavior is observed:

1. Printer Queue: When multiple documents are sent to a printer, they are stored 
in a print queue. The printer processes documents in the order they were sent 
(FIFO). The first document submitted is the first one printed. This ensures fair 
and orderly printing without any document being skipped.

2. Customer Service Call Centers: When customers call a service center, they are 
placed in a queue. The call system routes calls to available agents in the order 
they were received. The first customer to call may wait the longest but is also 
served first, maintaining fairness and predictability in the system.


Q4.2: Explain the four core queue operations: enqueue, dequeue, front, and 
isEmpty. For each, state its time complexity and justify it.

The four core queue operations are:

1. enqueue(val): Adds an element to the rear of the queue. In an array-based 
   implementation with a rear pointer, the element is placed at the rear position 
   and the rear pointer is incremented. In a linked list implementation, a new 
   node is created and attached to the rear node.
   Time Complexity: O(1) - Constant time because we only add one element at a 
   fixed position (the rear) without any traversal or searching.

2. dequeue(): Removes and returns the element from the front of the queue. In an 
   array-based implementation, this involves removing the front element and 
   incrementing the front pointer. In a linked list implementation, the front 
   node is removed and freed.
   Time Complexity: O(1) - Constant time because we only remove the element at 
   a fixed position (the front) directly, without traversing the queue.

3. front(): Returns the value of the front element without removing it from the 
   queue. This accesses the element at the front position.
   Time Complexity: O(1) - Constant time because we only read one element at a 
   fixed position (the front) directly, without any searching or traversal.

4. isEmpty(): Checks whether the queue is empty. This typically involves 
   comparing the front pointer to null or checking if front equals rear in a 
   specific way, returning a boolean.
   Time Complexity: O(1) - Constant time because it only checks a condition 
   (the front pointer value) regardless of queue size.


Q4.3: What is a circular queue? Why is it more memory-efficient than a simple 
linear array-based queue? Explain what happens to the front and rear pointers.

A circular queue is an improved array-based queue implementation where the rear 
end of the array wraps around to connect with the front end, forming a logical 
circle. This is implemented by using the modulo operator (%) to wrap indices 
around when they reach the end of the array.

Why Circular Queues are More Memory-Efficient:

In a simple linear queue, after multiple enqueue and dequeue operations, the 
front pointer moves forward but never goes backward. This causes "wasted space" 
at the beginning of the array that cannot be reused. For example, if we have a 
10-element array and dequeue 5 elements, the first 5 positions are now empty but 
cannot be used for new elementsâ€”new elements can only be added beyond the rear, 
wasting memory.

In a circular queue, when the rear pointer reaches the end of the array, it 
wraps around to the beginning using modulo arithmetic. This allows previously 
dequeued space at the front to be reused for new elements. Therefore, the same 
physical memory is recycled efficiently without waste.

How Front and Rear Pointers Work:

- Rear Pointer: When enqueuing, the rear pointer moves forward. When it reaches 
  the end of the array (size - 1), the next enqueue operation wraps it back to 
  index 0 using: rear = (rear + 1) % size

- Front Pointer: When dequeuing, the front pointer moves forward similarly. When 
  it reaches the end, it also wraps: front = (front + 1) % size

- Empty Check: The queue is empty when front == -1

- Full Check: The queue is full when (rear + 1) % size == front


Q4.4: Compare implementing a queue using an array vs a linked list. Which is 
generally preferred and why? Mention at least one scenario where the array 
version has a disadvantage.

Array-Based Queue:

Advantages:
- Memory Efficiency: Contiguous memory allocation, better cache locality, and no 
  per-element pointer overhead.
- Simple Implementation: Straightforward logic with index-based access.

Disadvantages:
- Fixed Capacity: Standard arrays have fixed size. Circular queues mitigate but 
  don't fully solve this limitation.
- Wasted Space in Linear Queues: In non-circular implementations, dequeued space 
  at the front cannot be reused, causing significant memory waste.

Linked List-Based Queue:

Advantages:
- Dynamic Size: Can grow without limit, only constrained by system memory.
- No Wasted Space: Memory is allocated only as needed; dequeued elements are 
  freed immediately.

Disadvantages:
- Memory Overhead: Each node carries pointer(s), increasing per-element memory 
  use significantly.
- Cache Inefficiency: Non-contiguous memory locations hurt CPU cache 
  performance.

Which is Generally Preferred:

For most use cases, linked list-based queues are preferred because:
1. They provide unlimited growth without manual resizing.
2. They eliminate memory waste from unused array slots.
3. They are more flexible when queue size is unpredictable.

Scenario Where Array Version Has Disadvantage:

Consider a system that processes variable-load tasks. During peak hours, 
10,000 tasks are queued; during quiet hours, only 100 tasks remain. With a 
fixed array of size 10,000, the quiet hours waste 9,900 array slots. A linked 
list queue would only allocate memory for the 100 tasks, making it far more 
memory-efficient during low-load periods.


